# Trap-recall questions. One per relocatable CLAUDE.md trap. `expect`: every regex must match the answer; `any`: each group needs one match; `reject`: none may match. Case-insensitive. Name the fact, never a bare negation or a generic word; a `reject` must skip negated forms ("not root's crontab"). `counter`: short plausible WRONG answers the grader must fail; `pass_example`: a real stored answer that passed. tests/test_context_eval_harness.py grades both.
{"id": "cli-db-envvar", "area": "patterns", "prompt": "In this repo, what must every CLI command's --db option carry, and why?", "expect": ["envvar\\s*=\\s*\\W?PHOTOSEARCH_DB|PHOTOSEARCH_DB.{0,80}(envvar|environment[ -]variable)|(envvar|environment[ -]variable).{0,80}PHOTOSEARCH_DB"], "reject": ["PHOTOSEARCH_DB\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,3}(deprecated|unused|ignored|not needed|optional)"], "counter": ["Every --db option needs default='photo_index.db' so commands find the database without extra setup.", "It carries a hardcoded default path; the PHOTOSEARCH_DB environment variable is deprecated and ignored."], "pass_example": "Every CLI command's `--db` option must carry **`envvar=\"PHOTOSEARCH_DB\"`**.\n\nThis is required because `PhotoDB()` with no argument opens a relative `photo_index.db` (which is an empty stub in the container); therefore, ad-hoc code must pass the path."}
{"id": "db-filename-schema", "area": "database", "prompt": "What is the SQLite database file called, and which constant in which file is the source of truth for the schema version?", "expect": ["photo_index\\.db", "SCHEMA_VERSION", "db\\.py"], "reject": ["(file|database|db)\\W+(is\\W+)?(called\\W+|named\\W+)?\\W?photos\\.db\\b", "\\bnot\\W+(in\\W+)?\\W?(photosearch/)?db\\.py"], "counter": ["The database file is called photos.db; SCHEMA_VERSION in photosearch/db.py is the source of truth (photo_index.db is an old name).", "It is photo_index.db; the schema version lives in migrations/VERSION as SCHEMA_VERSION, not in db.py."], "pass_example": "The SQLite database file is called `photo_index.db`.\n\nThe source of truth for the schema version is the `SCHEMA_VERSION` constant located in `db.py`."}
{"id": "frontend-no-build", "area": "frontend", "prompt": "Can frontend pages use JSX? What does a frontend change require before it shows up on the NAS?", "expect": ["React\\.createElement"], "any": [["(?<!no )(?<!not )(?<!n't )(?<!without )(?<!without a )\\brebuild", "docker\\W+compose\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,4}build", "(docker|container)\\W+image", "\\bimage\\W+(re)?build"]], "reject": ["^\\W*yes\\b", "(?<!no )(?<!not )\\bJSX\\W+(is\\W+)?(fine|supported|allowed|works)", "(?<!not )(?<!n't )(?<!cannot )\\bcan\\W+use\\W+JSX", "no\\W+(docker\\W+|image\\W+)?rebuild\\W+(is\\W+)?(needed|required)", "(just|only)\\W+(a\\W+)?(browser\\W+)?(refresh|reload)"], "counter": ["Yes, pages can use JSX (it compiles to React.createElement); a change needs only a browser refresh, no rebuild.", "JSX is supported via Babel standalone; React.createElement is optional. Just reload the page, no rebuild is needed."], "pass_example": "No, frontend pages cannot use JSX; they must use `React.createElement` because the frontend has no build step. \n\nA frontend change requires a **Docker image rebuild** before it shows up on the NAS."}
{"id": "sse-loop-and-events", "area": "patterns", "prompt": "For a long-running SSE endpoint in web.py, which asyncio call gets the loop, and which terminal event types must the stream always be able to emit?", "expect": ["get_running_loop", "\\bdone\\b", "\\bfatal\\b", "\\bcancell?ed\\b"], "reject": ["(?<!n't )(?<!not )(?<!never )\\b(use|call)s?\\s+(\\w+\\s+)?\\W?(asyncio\\.)?get_event_loop", "get_event_loop\\(\\)\\W*\\(?(not|rather than|instead of)\\W+\\W?(asyncio\\.)?get_running_loop"], "counter": ["Use asyncio.get_event_loop() (not get_running_loop()); the stream must be able to emit done, fatal and cancelled.", "asyncio.get_running_loop(); terminal events are progress and error."], "pass_example": "For long-running SSE endpoints in `photosearch/web.py`, you must use **`asyncio.get_running_loop()`** to obtain the loop. The stream must always be able to emit the terminal event types: **`done`**, **`fatal`**, and **`cancelled`**."}
{"id": "photodb-no-args", "area": "nas", "prompt": "In an ad-hoc Python snippet run inside the NAS photosearch container, what goes wrong if you write PhotoDB() with no arguments, and what should you pass?", "expect": ["PHOTOSEARCH_DB"], "any": [["/app/photo_index\\.db", "relative", "\\bstub\\b", "empty\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,2}(stub|database|db|file|sqlite)", "\\b(zero|0)\\W+(rows|photos|results|faces)", "(new|fresh|blank)\\W+(empty\\W+)?(database|db)\\b"]], "reject": ["nothing\\W+goes\\W+wrong", "PhotoDB\\(\\)\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,2}(reads|uses|picks up|honou?rs)\\W+\\W?PHOTOSEARCH_DB", "TypeError"], "counter": ["Nothing goes wrong: PhotoDB() reads PHOTOSEARCH_DB automatically and opens the real library, so no argument is needed.", "It raises a TypeError because the path argument is required; pass the relative path photo_index.db or PHOTOSEARCH_DB."], "pass_example": "If you call `PhotoDB()` with no arguments in an ad-hoc snippet inside the NAS container, it opens a relative `photo_index.db` file, which is an **empty stub** in that environment.\n\nTo fix this, you must pass the **absolute path** to the database, typically by reading it from the environment variable:\n`db = PhotoDB(os.environ['PHOTOSEARCH_DB'])`"}
{"id": "compose-entrypoint", "area": "nas", "prompt": "How do I run `python -c \"...\"` inside the photosearch container with docker compose on the NAS, and where must the entrypoint flag go?", "expect": ["--entrypoint"], "any": [["before\\W+(the\\W+)?(service|\\W?photosearch)", "--entrypoint\\W+python\\W+photosearch\\b", "docker-entrypoint", "cli\\.py"]], "reject": ["^\\W*put\\W+(the\\W+)?\\W?--entrypoint\\W+(\\w+\\W+)?after", "(?<!n't )(?<!not )(?<!cannot )(?<!never )\\b(goes|must\\W+come|comes)\\W+after\\W+(the\\W+)?(service|\\W?photosearch)"], "counter": ["Put --entrypoint after the service name: docker compose -f docker-compose.nas.yml run --rm photosearch --entrypoint python -c \"...\".", "Use docker compose exec photosearch python -c \"...\"; the --entrypoint flag can go anywhere in the command."], "pass_example": "To run `python -c \"...\"` inside the `photosearch` container on the NAS, use the following command structure:\n\n```bash\ndocker compose -f docker-compose.nas.yml run --rm --entrypoint python photosearch -c \"...\"\n```\n\nThe `--entrypoint` flag **must come before** the service name (`photosearch`)."}
{"id": "nas-git-safe-directory", "area": "nas", "prompt": "Why does a bare `git status` fail in the NAS checkout, and what is the one-line fix?", "expect": ["dubious\\W+ownership", "safe\\.directory"], "reject": ["safe\\.directory\\W+(is\\W+)?(not needed|unrelated|won't help|doesn't help|does not help)", "\\bnot\\W+(a\\W+|an\\W+)?\\W?dubious"], "counter": ["It fails because git is not installed on UGOS; install it with opkg. It is not a dubious ownership issue, so safe.directory won't help.", "Bare git fails with a permissions error; fix it with sudo chown -R $USER /volume1/docker/photosearch."], "pass_example": "A bare `git status` fails because the directory is owned by the NAS's system user (UGOS), triggering a \"dubious ownership\" security error in Git.\n\nThe one-line fix is:\n`git -c safe.directory=/volume1/docker/photosearch status`"}
{"id": "pgrep-self-match", "area": "patterns", "prompt": "Why must a watcher loop never poll `pgrep -f <pattern>`, and what should it watch instead?", "any": [["match\\w*\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,8}(itself|its own|own (process|command|argv|shell)|the watcher|the (watch|polling) loop|the loop)", "\\b(self|own)[- ](process|command|argv|match)", "\\bitself\\b"], ["marker", "\\bPID\\W+file|saved\\W+PID|\\$!|\\bkill -0", "terminal\\W+(SSE\\W+)?event", "\\.done\\b|done\\W+(file|marker)"]], "reject": ["pgrep -f\\W+(is|works)\\W+(fine|ok)", "(too\\W+)?slow\\b.{0,40}(pgrep|poll)|pgrep.{0,40}too\\W+slow"], "facts": "`pgrep -f <pattern>` also matches the watcher's own process/command line, so the loop always finds a match and never sees the job end. Watch something that cannot self-match instead: a marker/done file, `kill -0` on a PID saved at launch, or the job's terminal SSE event (done) in its log.", "counter": ["pgrep -f is too slow on the NAS; poll `ps aux | grep job` every second instead.", "pgrep -f is fine in itself, it just needs the full path; keep polling it with a longer sleep."], "pass_example": "A watcher loop must never poll `pgrep -f <pattern>` because it will match the watcher's own process, causing it to incorrectly detect that the target job is running.\n\nInstead, you should watch:\n- A **marker file**\n- A **saved PID** (using `kill -0`)\n- The job's **terminal SSE event**"}
{"id": "worker-d-photo-root", "area": "workers", "prompt": "What happens when the worker fleet is started with `-d /photos`, and what should I do to process the whole library?", "expect": ["404"], "any": [["(?<!n't )(?<!not )(?<!never )\\bomit", "without\\W+-d\\b", "leave\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,3}(out|off)", "drop\\W+(the\\W+)?\\W?-d\\b", "\\bno\\W+-d\\b"]], "reject": ["(don't|do not|never)\\W+omit", "trailing\\W+slash", "-d\\W+/photos/?\\W+(processes|covers|works)"], "facts": "`-d /photos` (the library root, not a real subdirectory) matches nothing: the server answers 404 'No photos found in directory /photos' and the fleet does no work. To process the whole library, omit -d entirely.", "counter": ["It returns a 404 at first and then retries; don't omit -d, pass -d /photos/ with a trailing slash.", "-d /photos processes the whole library; the 404 is only the health probe."], "pass_example": "When the worker fleet is started with `-d /photos`, it will **404** (return \"No photos found in directory /photos\") because the system expects a real subdirectory (like `/photos/2026`) rather than the photo root.\n\nTo process the whole library, you should **omit the `-d` flag entirely**."}
{"id": "worker-health-probe", "area": "workers", "prompt": "Which endpoint should a worker probe at startup to check the server is up, and why not /api/stats?", "expect": ["/api/health"], "any": [["117", "\\bslow", "\\bcold\\b", "expensive", "heavy", "(full|whole|entire)[- ]library", "count\\w*\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,4}(library|photos|rows|tables)", "\\bscan"]], "reject": ["(should|must|always)\\W+probe\\W+(GET\\W+)?/api/stats", "/api/health\\W+(is|runs|does)\\W+(\\w+\\W+)?(slow|expensive|heavy|a full)"], "facts": "Workers probe GET /api/health at startup (no DB work). /api/stats is wrong because it runs full-library counts that are slow/expensive, and the whole fleet would hit it at once.", "counter": ["Probe /api/stats: /api/health is slow and expensive because it scans the library.", "Workers should probe GET /api/stats; it is cached and cheap, and /api/health does not exist."], "pass_example": "A worker should probe `GET /api/health` at startup. \n\nThe `/api/stats` endpoint is not used because it costs 3 × 117 s on a cold NAS, and having every worker in a fleet probe it simultaneously would cause a significant performance hit."}
{"id": "tags-pass-removed", "area": "workers", "prompt": "Is `tags` a valid worker pass? If not, which passes replaced it?", "expect": ["category-visual", "category-content", "keywords"], "reject": ["^\\W*yes\\b", "\\btags\\W+is\\W+(still\\W+)?(a\\W+)?valid"], "counter": ["Yes, tags is still valid; it runs alongside category-visual, category-content and keywords."], "pass_example": "`tags` is **not** a valid worker pass. It's legacy (pre-v23) and was **replaced by three passes**:\n\n- `category-content` (categories)\n- `category-visual` (visual_tags)\n- `keywords` (keywords)\n\nSee photosearch/worker_api.py:1617-1620 (comment), and .claude/rules/workers.md (confirms `--passes tags` must keep erroring)."}
{"id": "aesthetics-primary-signal", "area": "llm", "prompt": "Which model scores the `aesthetics` worker pass by default, and why is the LAION aesthetic_score no longer the primary quality signal?", "expect": ["qwen2\\.5-vl"], "any": [["ceiling", "6\\.2", "6\\.9", "compress", "tops out"]], "reject": ["aesthetic_score\\W+(is|remains)\\W+(still\\W+)?(the\\W+)?primary", "qwen2\\.5-vl\\W+(was|is)\\W+(retired|removed|deprecated|no longer)"], "facts": "The `aesthetics` pass is scored by a VLM, by default Qwen2.5-VL (a qwen2.5-vl model). The LAION aesthetic_score is no longer the primary signal because it has a ceiling / compressed range (tops out around 6.2-6.9) that does not discriminate good photos; the VLM percentile (aes_overall_pct) is used for ranking instead.", "counter": ["llava scores it by default; qwen2.5-vl was retired because its scores hit a ceiling, and LAION aesthetic_score is still the primary signal.", "minicpm-v scores aesthetics; LAION was dropped because it is slow on the N100."], "pass_example": "**Model**: The `aesthetics` worker pass scores by default with **`qwen2.5-vl-7b-instruct`** (fleet default in `rerun.FLEET_ROLE_MODELS:106`).\n\n**Why LAION is no longer primary** (from `docs/notes/llm-passes.md:8`): The compressed LAION CLIP `aesthetic_score` had a 1–10 scale that topped out ~6.9 on curated Unsplash, so the library never exceeded ~6.2. The VLM replaces it as the primary signal; ranks use `aes_overall_pct` (percentile) not raw scores. LAION `aesthetic_score` remains as a cheap fallback prior for photos the VLM hasn't scored yet."}
{"id": "percentile-chunking", "area": "llm", "prompt": "Why does normalize-aesthetics write percentiles in 2000-row chunks, and what does the WHERE clause of each UPDATE guard on?", "any": [["\\block", "locked", "busy_timeout", "\\bwriters?\\b.{0,60}(block|wait|starv)"], ["(aes_overall|score_col\\}?|score\\W+column|source\\W+score)\\W*(IS|=)\\W*\\?", "\\bIS\\s+\\?", "source\\W+score", "score\\W+(it|the percentile)\\W+was\\W+derived", "old\\W+(value|score)", "\\b(unchanged|hasn't changed|not changed)\\b", "changed\\W+(concurrently|since|underneath|meanwhile)"]], "reject": ["no\\W+lock\\W+(issue|problem|contention)", "(nothing|not)\\W+to\\W+do\\W+with\\W+lock", "guards?\\W+(only\\W+)?on\\W+(the\\W+)?(photo\\W+)?id\\W*(only)?\\b(?!\\W+(and|plus|,))"], "facts": "normalize-aesthetics writes percentiles in 2000-row chunks so no single transaction holds the SQLite write lock for long (avoids 'database is locked' / busy_timeout failures for other writers). Each chunk's UPDATE is guarded on the source score the percentile was derived from, i.e. `WHERE id=? AND {score_col} IS ?` (the old value), so a row changed concurrently is not overwritten.", "counter": ["Chunks of 2000 keep memory bounded while loading scores; each UPDATE's WHERE clause guards only on the photo id.", "2000 is SQLite's maximum number of bound parameters; the WHERE guards on aes_overall_pct IS NULL so it only fills empty rows."], "pass_example": "**2000-row chunks:** Fixes `database is locked` failures when the GPU fleet submits results concurrently. Without chunking, the entire 158k–row percentile rewrite holds SQLite's writer lock for >60s on the N100; chunked writes (2000 rows / commit) leave frequent gaps for other writers and take <1s per chunk, even on spinning disks (`photosearch/aesthetics.py:421–430`).\n\n**WHERE clause guard:** Each UPDATE is guarded on the *source score* the percentile was derived from — `WHERE id=? AND {score_col} IS ?` (line 458). If a photo is rescored between the snapshot read and its chunk's write, the UPDATE finds `score_col` no longer matching and skips that row; it will pick up the correct percentile on the next run. This prevents silently writing a stale percentile. The code also skips rows whose stored percentile already matches (`if current != pct`), which avoids rewriting 158k unchanged rows on routine runs (`photosearch/aesthetics.py:440–448`)."}
{"id": "visual-derived-tags", "area": "llm", "prompt": "Which three visual tags are derived from EXIF instead of asked of the vision model, and what are the thresholds?", "expect": ["long-exposure", "low-light", "panoramic"], "any": [["1/4\\b", "0\\.25"], ["\\b5(\\.0)?\\b"], ["(aspect|ratio|edge).{0,60}\\b2(\\.0)?\\b", "\\b2(\\.0)?\\b.{0,40}(aspect|ratio)", "\\b2\\s*:\\s*1\\b"]], "facts": "Derived from EXIF, not asked of the model: long-exposure (exposure time >= 1/4 s, 0.25 s), low-light (EV100 below 5.0), panoramic (long edge / short edge aspect ratio >= 2.0).", "counter": ["long-exposure (shutter >= 1 s), low-light (ISO above 3200), panoramic (image width over 6000 px).", "long-exposure (shutter >= 1/4 s), low-light (EV100 below 10), panoramic (aspect ratio >= 3:1)."], "pass_example": "The three EXIF-derived tags are in `photosearch/visual_tags_derive.py` (the DERIVED group), computed server-side and authoritative over the model:\n\n- **`long-exposure`**: shutter ≥ **1/4 s**.\n- **`low-light`**: **EV100 < 5.0**, where EV100 = log2(N²/t) − log2(ISO/100).\n- **`panoramic`**: long/short edge aspect ratio ≥ **2.0**.\n\nMissing EXIF derives nothing. Derived terms always win in `merge_tags`, which strips any capture-fact term the model emitted. `motion-blur` is RETIRED, and `sharp`/`blurry` are FROZEN. None of those three is derived."}
{"id": "visual-frozen-sharp-blurry", "area": "llm", "prompt": "What is the status of the `sharp` and `blurry` visual tags: derived, asked of the model, or something else, and why?", "expect": ["frozen"], "any": [["aes_sharpness", "false\\W+positive", "unvalidated", "hand-label", "\\beval"]], "reject": ["(are|is)\\W+(now\\W+)?derived\\W+from\\W+(the\\W+)?\\W?(aes_)?sharpness", "\\bnot\\W+frozen"], "facts": "`sharp` and `blurry` are FROZEN tags: never derived, never asked of the model, never deleted (existing values pass through). Reason: the VLM's sharp/blurry judgement is unvalidated and prone to false positives; measured sharpness (aes_sharpness / the sharpness column) is the intended signal, pending a hand-labelled eval.", "counter": ["sharp and blurry are derived from aes_sharpness with fixed thresholds; they are not frozen.", "They are asked of the vision model like any other tag; only motion-blur is frozen."], "pass_example": "**FROZEN** — not asked of the model, not derived, and not deleted. `visual_tags_derive.py:87` defines `FROZEN_TAGS = frozenset({\"sharp\", \"blurry\"})`. \n\nThey were briefly derived from `aes_sharpness` (lines 62–79), but a backfill on the live DB caused **~50% false positives** (10,103 photos tagged `blurry` when at most two of four sampled were genuinely blurry). A native-resolution Laplacian was then tried (`sharpness.py`; see `docs/notes/llm-passes.md:246–251`) but no measured feature reached P ≥ 0.8 at R ≥ 0.5 — \"gradient energy cannot tell a soft *scene* from soft *focus*\". So the tags are frozen in all three directions: neither produced, requested, nor deleted, with 3,247 `sharp` and 1,001 `blurry` staying exactly as they are (delta 0)."}
{"id": "visual-retired-tag", "area": "llm", "prompt": "Which visual tag was retired from the vocabulary entirely and stripped from existing rows?", "expect": ["motion-blur"], "reject": ["motion-blur\\W+(was|is)\\W+(kept|still|retained)", "\\b(blurry|sharp)\\W+(was|is)\\W+(the\\W+)?(one\\W+)?retired"], "counter": ["`blurry` was retired and stripped from existing rows; motion-blur was kept."], "pass_example": "**`motion-blur`** was retired from the visual tag vocabulary entirely and stripped from existing rows. References: `docs/notes/llm-passes.md` and the test fixture `evals/context_eval/recall.jsonl` confirm this."}
{"id": "visual-derived-wins", "area": "llm", "prompt": "In the server-side visual-tag merge, what happens when the model emits `long-exposure` for a 1/800 s frame?", "any": [["(strip|drop|remov|discard)\\w*\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,6}(model|long-exposure|emitted|capture)", "(model|long-exposure|emitted)\\w*\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,6}(stripped|dropped|removed|discarded)", "derived\\W+wins", "EXIF\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,3}(wins|is\\W+authoritative|is\\W+the\\W+source\\W+of\\W+truth)"]], "reject": ["model('s)?\\W+(\\w+\\W+)?(wins|overrides)\\b", "model's\\W+(\\w+\\W+)?(tag\\W+)?(is\\W+)?(kept|trusted|preserved|retained)", "\\bunion\\b", "trusts?\\W+the\\W+(vision\\W+)?model"], "facts": "Derived wins: the server strips every capture-fact term the model emitted (so the model's `long-exposure` is dropped for a 1/800 s frame) because EXIF is authoritative; it strips even when nothing is derived, and missing EXIF derives nothing.", "counter": ["The model's long-exposure tag is kept and merged with the EXIF-derived tags (a union); the model wins on conflicts.", "Nothing special happens: the tag is stored as-is because the server trusts the vision model."], "pass_example": "In `merge_vlm_answer()` → `merge_tags()` at line 393, the model's `long-exposure` tag is **silently dropped**: it's in `CAPTURE_FACT_TAGS` (line 52), so it's excluded from `PERCEIVED_VOCABULARY` (lines 92–97), and `keep = incoming & set(PERCEIVED_VOCABULARY)` filters it out. \n\nThen at line 394, only EXIF-derived capture-fact tags are re-added: since 1/800 s is less than `LONG_EXPOSURE_MIN_SECONDS = 0.25` (line 217), `derive_tags()` line 311 doesn't add it back. The final result is an empty or perceived-only tag list—the model's contradictory answer is erased, not stored. **photosearch/visual_tags_derive.py:413–422 & 355–397** handle this; the guard in `describe.py` catches the contradiction before the write happens."}
{"id": "lmstudio-visual-role-env", "area": "llm", "prompt": "On the LM Studio (OpenAI-compatible) route, which environment variable selects the model for the category-visual pass, and which for category-content and keywords?", "expect": ["PHOTOSEARCH_LLM_VISUAL_MODEL", "PHOTOSEARCH_LLM_TEXT_MODEL"], "reject": ["TEXT_MODEL[^\\n;.|]{0,25}category-visual", "VISUAL_MODEL[^\\n;.|]{0,25}(category-content|keywords)", "category-visual[^\\n;.|]{0,25}PHOTOSEARCH_LLM_TEXT_MODEL", "(category-content|keywords)[^\\n;.|]{0,25}PHOTOSEARCH_LLM_VISUAL_MODEL"], "counter": ["PHOTOSEARCH_LLM_TEXT_MODEL selects category-visual; PHOTOSEARCH_LLM_VISUAL_MODEL selects category-content and keywords.", "PHOTOSEARCH_LLM_MODEL for every pass."], "pass_example": "From CLAUDE.md, the table under \"Routing LLM passes to LM Studio (OpenAI-compatible)\" shows:\n\n- **category-visual**: `PHOTOSEARCH_LLM_VISUAL_MODEL`\n- **category-content / keywords**: `PHOTOSEARCH_LLM_TEXT_MODEL`\n\nBoth passes select their model by **role** rather than by a call-site name on the LM Studio route. The text passes share the same `text` role and thus the same model env var."}
{"id": "reasoning-effort-none", "area": "llm", "prompt": "Why does the worker fleet need PHOTOSEARCH_LLM_REASONING_EFFORT=none for gemma-4 and minicpm, and what is the symptom without it?", "expect": ["none|reasoning_effort"], "any": [["reasoning", "thinking"], ["max_tokens", "(empty|blank)\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,3}(string|answer|response|output|content|reply|result)", "return\\w*\\W+(an\\W+)?(empty|'')", "''", "\"\"", "defer", "time\\W?out"]], "reject": ["(gemma|minicpm)\\w*\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,3}(aren't|are not|isn't|is not)\\W+(a\\W+)?reasoning", "they\\W+(aren't|are not)\\W+reasoning", "nothing\\W+(breaks|goes wrong)", "only\\W+(silences|suppresses)"], "facts": "The fleet's models are reasoning/thinking models. Without PHOTOSEARCH_LLM_REASONING_EFFORT=none they spend all of max_tokens on reasoning and return an empty string ''. Symptom: empty answers (passes defer or time out with nothing written).", "counter": ["They aren't reasoning models; the setting only silences a reasoning_effort warning. Without it nothing breaks and the answer is never empty.", "It makes the models faster; without it they are slower but give the same answers (reasoning_effort only affects speed)."], "pass_example": "**Why**: gemma-4 and minicpm-v-4_5 are reasoning models that think by default; without `reasoning_effort=none`, they spend most of their `max_tokens` budget on internal reasoning instead of generating the answer.\n\n**Symptom without it**: They return empty strings (`''`). Measured on 2026-10-03: gemma-4-12b spent 297 of 300 tokens reasoning and returned nothing; minicpm's backfill stalled forever (0 of 1,259 done) after burning its 768-token budget mid-thought. (See `photosearch/rerun.py` lines 120–124 and `describe.py` lines 714–716.)"}
{"id": "fleet-role-models-location", "area": "llm", "prompt": "Where in the code do the fleet's per-pass (per-role) default model names live, and how do you override one role for the UI-launched fleet?", "expect": ["FLEET_ROLE_MODELS", "rerun\\.py|rerun\\.FLEET|photosearch/rerun|\\brerun\\W+module"], "any": [["PHOTOSEARCH_FLEET_", "_fleet_env"]], "counter": ["In photosearch/worker.py as FLEET_ROLE_MODELS (rerun is unrelated); override one with PHOTOSEARCH_FLEET_TEXT_MODEL.", "FLEET_ROLE_MODELS in rerun.py; to override, edit the dict and restart the server."], "pass_example": "**Default models** live in `photosearch/rerun.py:103–116`, the `FLEET_ROLE_MODELS` dict (roles: `describe`, `verify`, `visual`, `aesthetics`, `text`, `subject`, `video`).\n\n**Override** a single role for the UI-launched fleet with `PHOTOSEARCH_FLEET_<ROLE>_MODEL` env var (e.g., `PHOTOSEARCH_FLEET_VERIFY_MODEL=google/gemma-4-e2b`). The resolution happens in `rerun.py:132` (`fleet_role_model()` function) and `admin_api._fleet_env()` applies it when spawning workers."}
{"id": "provenance-effective-model", "area": "llm", "prompt": "generations.model_used said `llava` for almost every category-visual row even though another model ran. Why, and which helper fixes it?", "expect": ["effective_model"], "any": [["\\brole", "nominal", "(configured|requested|default)\\W+(\\w+\\W+)?(model|name)", "\\bignor", "_resolve_openai_model", "(actually|really)\\W+(ran|served|answered|used)"]], "reject": ["effective_model\\W+(\\w+\\W+)?(is\\W+)?(unrelated|irrelevant|not the fix)", "\\btrigger\\b"], "counter": ["A DB trigger overwrote model_used with llava; drop the trigger. effective_model is unrelated, the configured model was correct.", "model_used is a cached display value; run the effective_model migration to refresh it."], "pass_example": "The problem is at **index.py:642 and 1443** — logging `model_used=category_visual_model` (the parameter) instead of the *actual* model that ran. When `tag_visual_photo()` loads a vision model via the LM Studio router, it may use a different model than the `category_visual_model` parameter (e.g., the env var `PHOTOSEARCH_LLM_VISUAL_MODEL` overrides it).\n\nThe fix is `describe.effective_model(model, role=\"visual\")` — it returns what actually ran. Look at the **Provenance rule** (line 39 of rules/llm-passes.md): stamp via `describe.effective_model()` / `effective_model_version` through `worker._provenance_kwargs()`. The worker already does this correctly; `index.py` needs the same call when logging category-visual generations."}
{"id": "text-pass-timeout", "area": "llm", "prompt": "What per-call timeout do the text-only Ollama passes (category-content, keywords) use, and what problem does it bound?", "expect": ["\\b10(\\.0)?\\W*(s|sec|secs|second|seconds)\\b|\\b10\\b.{0,60}_TEXT_OLLAMA_TIMEOUT|_TEXT_OLLAMA_TIMEOUT_S\\W+(=\\W*)?10\\b"], "any": [["stall", "stuck", "hang", "freez"]], "facts": "A tight per-call timeout of 10 s (`_TEXT_OLLAMA_TIMEOUT_S`) on the text-only Ollama passes. It bounds the Ollama stall problem (a call that hangs/freezes): the call gives up quickly and the photo is deferred rather than blocking.", "counter": ["100 seconds (_TEXT_OLLAMA_TIMEOUT_S); it bounds stuck calls.", "120 s, the Ollama default; it bounds slow model loads."], "pass_example": "**10 seconds** (`_TEXT_OLLAMA_TIMEOUT_S = 10` at `describe.py:244`). It bounds **Ollama stalls** on the text passes (`extract_categories_from_description` / `extract_keywords_from_description`): a timeout returns `None` to defer the photo (re-claimed later, no attempt spent) rather than blocking. See `.claude/rules/llm-passes.md`, last bullet."}
{"id": "attempts-cap", "area": "workers", "prompt": "After how many failed attempts does a worker pass stop claiming a photo, and which table and column track it?", "expect": ["worker_processed", "attempts"], "any": [["\\b3\\b", "\\bthree\\b"]], "reject": ["MAX_PROCESS_ATTEMPTS\\W*(=|is|of)\\W*(?!3\\b)\\d+\\b", "after\\W+(5|five|10|ten)\\W+(failed\\W+)?attempts"], "counter": ["After 5 failed attempts (MAX_PROCESS_ATTEMPTS = 5), tracked in worker_processed.attempts.", "It never stops; worker_processed.attempts is informational only (MAX_PROCESS_ATTEMPTS is unused)."], "pass_example": "**3 failed attempts.** The constant `MAX_PROCESS_ATTEMPTS = 3` (db.py:318) caps attempts; the table `worker_processed` column `attempts` tracks it. Claim queries exclude rows where `attempts >= MAX_PROCESS_ATTEMPTS`, and `mark_processed` UPSERT-increments on each failure."}
{"id": "server-write-failure-attempt", "area": "workers", "prompt": "When the NAS hits a transient `database is locked` while persisting a worker's submitted result, does the photo lose a retry attempt? What happens to the batch?", "any": [["^\\W*\\**no\\b", "(does not|doesn't|do not|don't|never|not|without)\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,3}(lose|losing|burn|spend|cost|consum|count|increment|charg)", "(no|without)\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,2}(attempt|retry)\\w*\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,2}(lost|burned|burnt|spent|consumed)", "attempts?\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,3}(unchanged|untouched|not\\W+(incremented|spent|burned))"], ["defer", "releas", "re-?claim"]], "reject": ["^\\W*\\**yes\\b", "\\b(lock\\w*|it|photo)\\W+(\\w+\\W+){0,2}(burns|loses|spends|costs|consumes)\\W+(an?|one)\\W+(\\w+\\W+)?(attempt|retry)"], "facts": "No: a transient 'database is locked' on the server's write must not burn a retry attempt. The batch is deferred (status 'deferred', deferred_photo_ids) and its claim released so the photos are re-claimed later.", "counter": ["Yes, it burns one attempt like any failure; the batch is deferred and re-claimed later.", "The photo loses an attempt and the whole batch is marked failed."], "pass_example": "Based on the code in `worker_api.py`, **the photo does NOT lose a retry attempt** when the NAS hits a `database is locked` error during result submission:\n\n1. **No attempt spent**: `_record_write_failure` (line 772) checks `_is_transient_db_error` (line 704). A `sqlite3.OperationalError` containing \"locked\" or \"busy\" (line 701: `_TRANSIENT_DB_MARKERS`) returns `True`, calling `outcome.defer(photo_id)` (line 783) instead of `outcome.failed(photo_id)`. **The photo keeps its attempt count** (comment line 651).\n\n2. **Batch handling**: If the lock happens at the batch COMMIT level, `_commit_batch` (line 799) defers the entire batch (line 828). The `status` response includes `\"deferred_photo_ids\"` (line 1514); the worker claim is released but **nothing is marked processed**, so the batch comes back on the next claim.\n\n3. **Result**: The photo stays claimable (claim released, attempt count unchanged) and is reclaimed on a later pass. Verified in test `test_video_probe_submit.py:183` — a lock defers the photo, leaves `worker_processed` empty, and keeps the photo claimable."}
{"id": "faces-empty-terminal", "area": "workers", "prompt": "What does the server do with a clean `faces` result that found nobody in the photo, and why is that safe?", "any": [["terminal", "straight\\W+to\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,3}(cap|max|attempt|exhaust)", "(jump|set|mark|push)\\w*\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,5}(cap|MAX_PROCESS_ATTEMPTS|exhaust)", "(never|not)\\W+(be\\W+)?(re-?claimed|retried)"], ["exception\\w*\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,10}(failure|error\\W+row|failures)", "(failure|error)\\W+rows?", "failures\\W+list", "FailedResult", "never\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,6}(faces:\\s*\\[\\]|empty)", "(error|exception)\\w*\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,4}(spends?|spent|counts?|burns?)\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,2}attempt"]], "reject": ["(?<!not )(?<!never )(?<!n't )(?<!never be )(?<!not be )re-?claim(ed|s)?\\W+(later|again|next)"], "facts": "A clean empty `faces` result is TERMINAL: mark_processed(terminal=True) jumps the photo straight to the attempts cap, so it is not re-claimed. This is safe only because a detection exception is recorded as a failure row, never as `faces: []`.", "counter": ["The server retries it up to MAX_PROCESS_ATTEMPTS times since an empty result may hide a failure; that is safe because retries are cheap.", "It is stored as an exception failure and re-claimed later by the next worker."], "pass_example": "When a `faces` result comes back with an empty list `[]`, the server calls `outcome.finished_empty()` which marks it with `terminal=True`. This makes `db.mark_processed(..., terminal=True)` jump the photo straight to the attempts cap (`MAX_PROCESS_ATTEMPTS` = 3), retiring it in **one pass** instead of re-claiming it repeatedly.\n\n**Why it's safe:** A detection *exception* always gets reported as a `FailedResult` in the `failures` list—never as an empty `faces` array. So an empty list is guaranteed to be a **real result** (the model looked, found nobody). Without this optimization, re-detecting the same empty photo up to 3 times would waste ~136k InsightFace runs library-wide per library-wide reset. Lines 1049–1055 in `worker_api.py` enforce the contract; the `worker._process_faces` function must never submit `faces: []` on error."}
{"id": "video-nas-never-decodes", "area": "video", "prompt": "Does the NAS ever probe or decode video files? Who does, and what tool does that require?", "expect": ["ffprobe"], "any": [["^\\W*\\**no\\b", "NAS\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,3}(never|does not|doesn't|does no|cannot|can't|won't)\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,3}(probe|decode|run|touch|ffprobe|ffmpeg)", "never\\W+(probes|decodes|touches)", "(NAS|server)\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,3}(only\\W+)?(stores|serves)"], ["worker", "desktop", "native"]], "reject": ["^\\W*\\**yes\\b", "NAS\\W+(itself\\W+|also\\W+|then\\W+)?(runs|probes|decodes|calls|uses)\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,2}(ffprobe|ffmpeg|video)"], "facts": "No: the NAS never probes or decodes video. The workers do, on a native machine (the desktop worker, not the Docker fleet), which requires ffprobe (and ffmpeg) installed.", "counter": ["Yes, the NAS runs ffprobe on every new video at ingest; the workers only describe frames.", "The NAS probes video with ffprobe during the maintenance sweep, not the workers."], "pass_example": "**No.** The NAS never decodes or probes video files (owner decision D19, per `.claude/rules/video.md` line 14). It walks the tree, inserts rows, stores uploads, and runs Live-pairing SQL only.\n\n**Worker passes do the probing:** `video-probe` and `video-proxy` passes run on workers with `--native` flag. They require:\n- **`ffprobe`** (via `video_probe.find_ffprobe`)  \n- **`ffmpeg`** (via `video_proxy.find_ffmpeg`)\n\nBoth discovered in order: `PHOTOSEARCH_FFPROBE`/`PHOTOSEARCH_FFMPEG` env vars, then `PATH`, then `~/.local/bin`. A pass drops itself if missing. The static ffprobe segfaults on `localhost`—point workers at `127.0.0.1` (line 16)."}
{"id": "pass-media-map", "area": "video", "prompt": "What is db.PASS_MEDIA for, and what happens to a worker pass that is missing from it?", "expect": ["raise|KeyError|ValueError"], "any": [["\\bstill", "video", "\\bimage", "media[_ ]type"]], "reject": ["(does not|doesn't|won't|never|not)\\W+(\\w+\\W+)?raise", "silently\\W+(skip|ignor|default)"], "counter": ["It maps each worker pass to the media types (still/video) it applies to; a pass missing from it is silently skipped, it does not raise.", "It lists the passes the NAS runs locally; a missing pass runs on every media type."], "pass_example": "`db.PASS_MEDIA` (line 186 in `photosearch/db.py`) maps each worker pass to its applicable media type (\"still\", \"video\", or \"any\"), controlling which rows each pass may claim. A pass without an entry raises `KeyError` deliberately (line 222: `media_sql()` does `MEDIA_SQL[PASS_MEDIA[pass_type]]`). The test `test_every_worker_pass_has_a_media_entry()` in `tests/test_pass_media.py:76–79` enforces this: it iterates all `WORKER_PASSES`, `worker_api._ALL_PASSES`, and `rerun.ALL_PASSES`, so a new pass cannot forget its `PASS_MEDIA` entry without CI failing."}
{"id": "video-passes-native", "area": "video", "prompt": "Why must the four video worker passes run with --native rather than in the Docker worker fleet?", "expect": ["ffprobe\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,6}(not|isn't|missing|absent|lacks|no)\\b|\\b(not|isn't|missing|absent|lacks|lack|no|without)\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,6}ffprobe|segfault"], "any": [["docker", "image", "container"]], "reject": ["ffprobe\\W+(is\\W+)?(already\\W+)?(in|inside|included|installed\\W+in|shipped\\W+in)\\W+(the\\W+)?(docker|worker|container|image)"], "counter": ["Because the Docker image has no GPU access for decoding; ffprobe is in the image but too slow there.", "Native runs are faster for ffmpeg; the Docker image works too, just slower."], "pass_example": "ffprobe and ffmpeg are not in the Docker worker image (`.claude/rules/video.md` line 16). The passes discover them via `PHOTOSEARCH_FFPROBE` / `PHOTOSEARCH_FFMPEG` environment variables, PATH, or `~/.local/bin`; if missing, the pass drops itself. Additionally, the static ffprobe segfaults resolving `localhost`, so native workers must point to `127.0.0.1` instead. The five video passes (`video-probe`, `video-proxy`, `video-scan`, `video-subjects`, `video-describe`) are the only ones that need this since all other passes run on still images only."}
{"id": "clip-reclaim-cap", "area": "workers", "prompt": "What was the clip-claim infinite re-claim bug, and how is clip capped today?", "any": [["unloadable", "could not load", "cannot identify", "\\bZIP", "no trace", "no ledger", "never.{0,60}mark_processed"], ["_CLIP_NOT_EXHAUSTED", "attempts?\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,4}(ledger|cap|MAX_PROCESS_ATTEMPTS|spend|spent|3\\b)", "(failure|error)\\W+rows?", "ledger", "MAX_PROCESS_ATTEMPTS"]], "reject": ["\\b(has|have)\\W+no\\W+cap\\b", "(attempts|ledger)\\W+(are|is)\\W+not\\W+used"], "facts": "Unloadable/non-image files (e.g. ZIP-wrapped .JPG Live Photo bundles) never produced an embedding or any ledger row, so the clip pass re-claimed them forever. Now clip is on the attempts ledger: unloadable images come back as failure rows that spend attempts, and the claim/count predicates carry `_CLIP_NOT_EXHAUSTED` (cap MAX_PROCESS_ATTEMPTS = 3); a successful embedding is its own marker.", "counter": ["Unloadable files were re-claimed because of a race between workers; clip has no cap today and attempts are not used for clip.", "Clip re-claimed already-embedded photos because of a missing index; it is capped by a 24 h cooldown."], "pass_example": "**The bug:** Unloadable files (ZIP-wrapped iOS Live Photos saved as `.JPG`, PIL couldn't open) never received a CLIP embedding. Without a ledger or attempt cap, CLIP re-claimed them forever — each sat at the front of the queue indefinitely.\n\n**Capped today:** CLIP now tracks attempts in `worker_processed` table per the `_CLIP_NOT_EXHAUSTED` predicate: `NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM worker_processed WHERE photo_id = p.id AND pass_type = 'clip' AND attempts >= MAX_PROCESS_ATTEMPTS)`. After 3 failures, a photo is retired; `mark_processed` increments the counter. See `db.py:332–336` and `cli.py:2113–2115`."}
{"id": "ingest-no-index-trap", "area": "ingest", "prompt": "Why must you never run `ingest-incoming --no-index` to skip CLIP, and what flag should you use instead?", "expect": ["--no-clip"], "any": [["(insert|creat|regist|writ|add)\\w*\\W+no\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,2}rows?", "\\bno\\W+(DB|database|photos)\\W+rows?", "unclaimable", "unsearchable", "invisible", "(never|not)\\W+(be\\W+)?(indexed|registered)", "rows?\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,3}(never|not)\\W+(inserted|created|registered|written)", "\\bno\\W+rows?\\W+(in|for)\\b", "never\\W+inserts?", "can't\\W+claim"]], "reject": ["--no-index\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,2}(is|works)\\W+(fine|ok|safe)", "no\\W+rows?\\W+(are|is)\\W+lost"], "facts": "`--no-index` makes ingest insert no DB rows at all (ingest itself inserts none), so the moved files are unclaimable by workers, unsearchable and invisible to hash dedup. Use `--no-clip` instead: it registers rows and leaves CLIP to the fleet.", "counter": ["--no-index just skips CLIP and is safe but slower; use --no-clip for speed. No rows are lost.", "--no-index also skips the EXIF pass, so dates are missing; use --no-clip."], "pass_example": "From `.claude/rules/ingest.md` line 15:\n\n**Never use `--no-index`** — `ingest` itself inserts no rows, so `--no-index` leaves moved files unclaimable, unsearchable, and invisible to hash dedup.\n\n**Use `--no-clip` instead** — it registers rows in the database while deferring CLIP embedding to the worker fleet."}
{"id": "refile-link-not-rename", "area": "ingest", "prompt": "Why does refile-unknown-camera move files with os.link + unlink instead of os.rename or shutil.copy2?", "expect": ["EEXIST"], "any": [["silently\\W+(overwrit|clobber|replac)", "(overwrit|clobber|replac)\\w*\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,4}(existing|destination|target|file|whatever)", "(prevent|avoid|never|without)\\w*\\W+(\\w+\\W+)?(overwrit|clobber)"]], "reject": ["nothing\\W+to\\W+do\\W+with\\W+overwrit"], "counter": ["os.rename can't cross filesystems and copy2 is slow; os.link raises EEXIST on cross-device moves. Nothing to do with overwriting files.", "Hardlinks keep the inode so Syncthing doesn't re-upload; EEXIST is ignored."], "pass_example": "**Atomicity and race-safety.** `os.link` fails with `EEXIST` if destination exists (line 228), preventing overwrites, while `os.rename` and `shutil.copy2` silently clobber (lines 22–25, 211–217 in `refile.py`). The race is real: nightly ingest cron and the SD-card importer write into these same dated folders simultaneously, and Sony DSC filenames repeat across cards, creating collisions. `os.link + os.unlink` provides non-overwriting semantics at the syscall level. A crash between link and unlink leaves both names on one inode—recoverable as `duplicate_left` on the next run (lines 218–220). The fallback cross-device copy path uses `O_EXCL` for the same guarantee (line 255)."}
{"id": "maintenance-cron-time", "area": "maintenance", "prompt": "Where does the nightly maintenance-sweep cron entry live on the NAS, and at what time does it run?", "any": [["18:30", "30 18", "6:30 ?pm"], ["crontab", "cantimatt", "user('s)?\\W+(cron|crontab)"]], "reject": ["(?<!not )\\b(lives|sits|(it|entry)\\W+is)\\W+in\\W+(the\\W+)?(root('s)?\\W+crontab|/etc/cron)", "^\\W*in\\W+(the\\W+)?(root|/etc/cron)"], "facts": "The nightly `maintenance-sweep --apply --no-colors` cron entry lives in the NAS user's crontab (flock-guarded) and runs daily at 18:30.", "counter": ["In /etc/cron.d/photosearch (root) at 02:00 nightly.", "In the root crontab, at 18:30."], "pass_example": "The `maintenance-sweep` cron entry lives in **the NAS user's own crontab** (on the NAS itself) and runs at **18:30 UTC** (6:30 PM). The entry is: `30 18 * * * … flock -n /tmp/photo-maintenance.lock … maintenance-sweep --apply --no-colors …` (see `.claude/skills/photo-search/reference/nas-operations.md:384–390`)."}
{"id": "sweep-match-strict-only", "area": "maintenance", "prompt": "Does the nightly maintenance sweep run the temporal face matcher? How would you turn it on?", "any": [["strict[- ]only", "only\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,2}strict", "^\\W*\\**no\\b", "opt-?in", "(does not|doesn't|not|never)\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,3}(run|use|include)\\w*\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,3}temporal"], ["--match-temporal", "match_temporal\\s*=\\s*True"]], "reject": ["^\\W*\\**yes\\b", "--no-match-temporal", "(runs|includes|uses)\\W+(both|the\\W+temporal)"], "facts": "No: the sweep's match_faces stage is STRICT-ONLY. Temporal matching is opt-in: pass --match-temporal (match_temporal=True); never on by default.", "counter": ["Yes, it runs both the strict and the temporal matcher; turn temporal off with --no-match-temporal.", "No, temporal matching is disabled in code and cannot be turned on."], "pass_example": "No. The nightly sweep's `match_faces` stage runs **STRICT-ONLY** matching (per `.claude/rules/maintenance.md`). Temporal matching is gated behind `--match-temporal`, an opt-in flag in `cli.py:2565`, disabled by default because it over-matches on kids' sports—the sweep runs unattended before any photo review.\n\nTo enable it: `photosearch maintenance-sweep --apply --match-temporal`. Or programmatically: pass `match_temporal=True` to `run_maintenance_sweep()` in `photosearch/maintenance.py`."}
{"id": "exclusions-churn-fix", "area": "faces", "prompt": "The sweep's match_faces and resolve_dups stages used to apply the same ~6000 faces every night. What table stops that loop and what does it remember?", "expect": ["face_person_exclusions"], "any": [["\\bpair", "which\\W+person", "face(_id)?\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,3}(and|,|\\+|→|->|/|×|x)\\W*(\\w+\\W+)?person", "person_id"]], "counter": ["face_person_exclusions stores the timestamp of the last sweep so the stages skip nights with no new photos.", "face_person_exclusions remembers which faces were already shown to the user."], "pass_example": "`face_person_exclusions` stops the loop. It remembers the (face, person) pairing that `resolve_dups` stripped — the unmatches. Both `match_faces` (STRICT-only) and temporal matching consult it via `faces.load_match_exclusions` (loaded once per run, not per-face). This prevents the sweep from re-matching faces that were already deduped as duplicates the night before."}
{"id": "exclusion-strict-vs-temporal", "area": "faces", "prompt": "When a face's best match is a person barred by face_person_exclusions, how do the strict and the temporal matchers behave differently, and why must they not be unified?", "any": [["strict\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,12}(next|fall|runner)", "fall\\w*.?through", "next[- ]best"], ["temporal\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,14}(unmatched|leaves|refuses|no\\W+label|no\\W+match)", "leaves?\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,4}unmatched", "min_gap", "ambigu"]], "reject": ["both\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,3}(skip|fall|take)\\w*\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,4}next", "strict\\W+(\\w+\\W+){0,3}leaves?\\W+(\\w+\\W+){0,3}unmatched", "temporal\\W+(matcher\\W+|one\\W+)?(?<!not )(?<!n't )falls?\\W+through\\W+to", "(should|can|could)\\W+be\\W+unified"], "facts": "Strict matcher: if the best match is barred by face_person_exclusions it falls through to the next in-tolerance person. Temporal matcher: it ranks the UNFILTERED field and leaves the face unmatched if the winner is barred. They must not be unified because filtering first would defeat TEMPORAL_MIN_GAP (the ambiguity gap) and promote ambiguous siblings.", "counter": ["Both matchers skip the barred person and take the next best match, so nothing is left unmatched; they could be unified.", "The strict matcher leaves the face unmatched; the temporal one falls through to the next person. No reason they differ."], "pass_example": "**Strict falls through to the next in-tolerance person; temporal does NOT.**\n\nWhen a face's best match is barred by `face_person_exclusions`:\n\n- **Strict matcher** (`match_faces_to_persons`): Continues ranking and matches the next candidate that meets its distance threshold — safe because tolerance is an absolute check against the threshold.\n- **Temporal matcher** (`match_faces_temporal`): Leaves the face unmatched (`person_id = NULL`) — the barred candidate is not removed from ranking before the judgment.\n\n**Why they can't be unified** (per `test_temporal_matcher_does_NOT_fall_through_to_the_next_best_person`): Temporal's `TEMPORAL_MIN_GAP` judgment compares the best candidate against *the whole field*. If you filtered out the barred best candidate before judging, the runner-up would get an unopposed win the unfiltered field never gave them, violating the semantics of the gap check. Strict has no such global comparison — it just checks absolute tolerance — so filtering is safe and consistent.\n\n**Code reference:** `.claude/rules/faces.md:18`, `tests/test_face_person_exclusions.py:289–309`."}
{"id": "rejected-match-source", "area": "faces", "prompt": "What does match_source='rejected' mean, why does it exist when dedupe_unmatched already did, and how do the matchers honour it?", "expect": ["rejected"], "any": [["MATCHABLE_SQL", "re-?appl", "matcher\\w*\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,6}(skip|exclude|filter)", "filtered\\W+out"], ["dedupe_unmatched"]], "reject": ["(same\\W+as|alias\\W+(of|for)|identical\\W+to)\\W+(\\w+\\W+)?\\W?dedupe_unmatched", "(rejected|it)\\W+(is\\W+)?(just\\W+)?an?\\W+alias"], "facts": "match_source='rejected' is a human 'no' written when a human clears a label, so it survives auto-matching (NULL would read as never-matched and the next sweep would re-apply the match). dedupe_unmatched is different: it stays matchable because the loser of a duplicate may be a different person. Both matchers honour it via the shared faces.MATCHABLE_SQL predicate (they filter it out).", "counter": ["'rejected' is just an alias of dedupe_unmatched kept for old rows; the matchers re-apply both on the next sweep.", "rejected marks faces the detector threw away as non-faces; matchers ignore dedupe_unmatched."], "pass_example": "`match_source='rejected'` records a **human \"no\"** to a match suggestion. It prevents auto-matching a face to *any* person via the shared `MATCHABLE_SQL` predicate (`f.match_source <> 'rejected'`) used by both strict and temporal matchers (faces.py:1129, 1136). \n\nIt exists distinctly from `'dedupe_unmatched'` because deduplication's losing face may legitimately match a *different* person—only a human rejection is absolute. Written by `unmatch-person` and face-state clears; never set to NULL (NULL reads as never-matched, triggering re-application on next sweep). Shared with `face_suggest.py:75` so all match paths respect it uniformly."}
{"id": "rival-test", "area": "faces", "prompt": "What is the face-label rival test, and why can it find errors the margin test structurally cannot?", "expect": ["same photo"], "any": [["unlabel", "untagged", "not\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,3}registered", "\\bswap"]], "reject": ["\\bnot\\W+(in\\W+)?the\\W+same\\W+photo", "(in|from)\\W+other\\W+photos\\b"], "facts": "The rival test asks whether the right person's face is another face in the SAME PHOTO (typically an unlabelled face, or one not registered as that person, so a swap is possible). It can find errors the margin test structurally cannot because it looks at the other faces in the same photo rather than only comparing the labelled face against references.", "counter": ["It compares the face against faces of the same person in other photos (not the same photo); the margin test can't because it only uses one reference.", "It checks the label against the person's average embedding; margin can't because it ignores swaps."], "pass_example": "The **rival test** (`face_verify._photo_rivals`, shipped 2026-09-16) flags when another face in the *same photo* is closer to a person's reference faces than the labeled face itself. It runs four gates: one-to-one matching (via `linear_sum_assignment`), rivals closer than incumbent, distance gates around `radius(P)` (p90 of genuine P's distance), and no competing claimants.\n\n**Structural difference:** The margin test needs the true identity as a registered person with references to compare against — it cannot detect an error if the better claimant is *unlabelled* (has no person_id). The rival test doesn't: it just asks \"is there a better face here in this frame?\" On two soccer shoots (1,895 photos), the margin test fired 108 findings (96 temporal noise); rivals filtered to **1 true error** and proved it with a one-click Swap. See `faces.md:face-label integrity` (margin path), `faces.md:the rival test` (gates and radius calibration), and memory `[[project_face_rival_test]]` (why p90 near percentile-independent, burst-alibi pollution risk)."}
{"id": "leave-one-burst-out", "area": "faces", "prompt": "Why does verify-face-labels exclude the whole burst rather than just the face itself when measuring a face against its person's references?", "expect": ["burst"], "any": [["impostor", "vouch", "same\\W+(mis)?label|same\\W+mistake|share\\W+the\\W+(mis)?label|carry\\W+the\\W+same", "(other|sibling|neighbo\\w*)\\W+(\\w+\\W+){0,2}frames?", "near[- ]identical|near[- ]duplicate|nearly\\W+identical", "\\b(1|one)[ -]?(s|sec|second)\\b", "collapse"]], "reject": ["(speed|perf\\w*)\\W+optimi[sz]", "to\\W+save\\W+time"], "facts": "A face's burst siblings (frames about one second apart) carry the same mislabel and would vouch for it (the impostor looks like its own reference). Excluding only the face itself leaves those near-identical frames, which collapses the distance; excluding the whole burst (BURST_WINDOW_SECONDS) measures it against independent references.", "counter": ["Excluding the whole burst is a speed optimisation: it skips about 1 s of comparisons per face.", "Because burst frames are blurry, so they would add noise to the reference set."], "pass_example": "Looking at `photosearch/face_verify.py:199-200` and the `_mask()` function: when measuring a face against its person's references, a burst sibling with the **same mislabel** could still vouch for the incorrect label, defeating the check. Excluding the whole burst ensures you're comparing against independent observations. The comment is explicit: `# same burst — cannot vouch for it`.\n\nThis is why the rule states: \"exclude the face's whole burst (BURST_WINDOW_SECONDS), not just the face: **a burst sibling with the same mislabel vouches for it**\" (`.claude/rules/faces.md`, Face-label integrity bullet)."}
{"id": "ps-poll-not-setinterval", "area": "frontend", "prompt": "How should a frontend page poll a status endpoint, and why is setInterval wrong here?", "expect": ["PS\\.poll"], "any": [["settle", "previous\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,3}(request|call|poll|fetch|one|promise)", "came\\W+back", "(has|have|had)\\W+(not\\W+)?returned|before\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,4}returns?", "in-flight", "stack|pile|overlap|queue\\W+up", "40\\W+(request\\W+)?threads", "starv"]], "reject": ["(?<!when )(?<!when `)setInterval`?\\W+(is\\W+)?(fine|ok|okay)\\b(?!\\W+(only|for))", "too\\W+slow"], "facts": "Use PS.poll(fn, ms). setInterval fires whether or not the previous request has returned, so on a starved NAS requests stack up and fill the server's request threads (the status page wedged the box it reported on). PS.poll re-arms only after fn's promise settles, pauses while the tab is hidden, and stop() aborts the in-flight call.", "counter": ["Use PS.poll; setInterval is wrong because it fires too slowly in previous browsers.", "Wrap setInterval in PS.poll for backward compatibility; setInterval is fine but leaks memory in hidden tabs."], "pass_example": "Use **`PS.poll(fn, ms)`** from `shared.js` (line 17 of `.claude/rules/frontend.md`): it re-arms only *after* `fn`'s promise settles, pauses when the tab is hidden, and returns `stop()` to abort in-flight calls. Your `fn(signal)` must **return** the fetch chain.\n\n`setInterval` fires whether or not the last request returned—on a starved NAS it filled every request thread and wedged the status page itself. `setInterval` is fine only for pure UI ticks (TTL countdowns). Tests are in `frontend/__tests__/poll.test.js`."}
{"id": "frontend-ref-check", "area": "frontend", "prompt": "The frontend has no linter. What catches a helper that is called from a page which never defines it?", "expect": ["check-frontend-refs"], "reject": ["check-frontend-refs\\S*\\W+(\\w+\\W+)?(was|is|has been)\\W+(removed|deleted|deprecated)", "(?<!no )(?<!not )(?<!without )\\bESLint\\W+(catches|flags|reports)"], "counter": ["ESLint's no-undef rule catches it; scripts/check-frontend-refs.js was removed.", "Nothing does; the page just throws a ReferenceError in the browser."], "pass_example": "`scripts/check-frontend-refs.js` — run with `node scripts/check-frontend-refs.js` or `cd frontend && npm run check:refs`. It catches bare function calls that a page makes but never defines, and also validates that `PS.*` calls reference members actually defined in `shared.js`. The historical bug it was written for: `parseSSEChunk` defined only in `admin_maintenance.html` shipped in `faces.html` (lines 1–16 document why syntax checks alone miss this)."}
{"id": "restart-helper-container", "area": "frontend", "prompt": "Why can't /api/admin/restart run `docker compose up` for photosearch from inside the photosearch container, and what does it do instead?", "expect": ["helper"], "any": [["PID\\W+namespace", "\\bdies\\b|\\bdie\\b|killed|\\bkills?\\b", "strand", "Created\\W+(state|status)|stuck\\W+in\\W+\\W?Created", "(own|its own|the old)\\W+container\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,4}(stop|remov|replac|torn|kill)"]], "reject": ["docker\\W+socket\\W+(isn't|is\\W+not)\\W+mounted", "(no|without)\\W+(the\\W+)?docker\\W+socket"], "counter": ["It can't because the docker socket isn't mounted in the container; a helper shell script on the host created the new container instead.", "It runs docker compose up in a background thread; the helper only adds logging."], "pass_example": "**Why:** All processes in a container share one PID namespace. When Docker SIGKILLs the container during recreation, the compose subprocess dies *between* \"old container dead\" and \"new container started\", stranding the new container in Created state. `setsid`/`nohup` can't escape this (same PID namespace).\n\n**What it does instead** (`admin_api.py:1034–1042`): Launches a detached `docker:cli` helper container (`photosearch-restart-helper`) with:\n- Its own PID namespace (separate lifecycle from photosearch)\n- Mounts to `/var/run/docker.sock` and `HOST_REPO_DIR` \n- Runs `docker compose -p $PROJECT -f docker-compose.nas.yml up -d --no-deps photosearch`\n- The helper exits after compose finishes; the new photosearch container is already live\n\nAlso extends active worker claims by 15 minutes before launching the helper (`admin_api.py:1014–1023`) so in-flight batches survive the swap gap."}
{"id": "batch-zero-unprocessed-lies", "area": "ingest", "prompt": "In batch_state, why does count_unprocessed_photos returning 0 not mean a step is completed? Name the two cases.", "any": [["exhaust", "\\bblocked\\b", "attempts?\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,4}(cap|max|MAX_PROCESS_ATTEMPTS|used\\W+up|spent)"], ["\\bwaiting\\b", "description\\W+IS\\W+NOT\\W+NULL", "gated", "(earlier|upstream|prior|previous|dependent|prerequisite)\\W+(\\w+\\W+){0,2}(step|pass|stage)", "not\\W+(yet\\W+)?(eligible|claimable)"]], "reject": ["cach(e|ed|es)\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,3}result"], "facts": "count_unprocessed_photos is a claim predicate, not progress, so 0 does not mean completed. Case 1: every photo is exhausted (attempts at the cap), which reads `blocked`. Case 2: the step is gated/waiting on an earlier step (e.g. no description yet), which reads `waiting`. `completed` means done == total.", "counter": ["Because count_unprocessed_photos caches its result; describe runs later and attempts reset.", "Case 1: the photos were deleted. Case 2: the worker crashed."], "pass_example": "**Blocked** and **waiting**.\n\n`count_unprocessed_photos` returns 0 when (1) the pass is genuinely finished, (2) **every remaining photo has exhausted attempts** (blocked), and (3) **the pass is gated on an input that doesn't exist yet** (waiting). Cases 2 and 3 read as `remaining == 0` but the step is not complete — `done == total` is the actual completion check (batch_state.py:35, 446)."}
{"id": "batch-queued-source", "area": "ingest", "prompt": "Where does a batch step's `queued` state come from, and what must it never be derived from?", "expect": ["ingest_batch_jobs"], "any": [["fleet-status", "run-workers\\.sh --status", "process list"]], "reject": ["(?<!never )(?<!not )(?<!never be )(?<!not be )(?<!n't be )\\b(comes|derived|read)\\W+from\\W+(the\\W+)?\\W?(fleet-status|run-workers\\.sh\\W+--status|process\\W+list)", "ingest_batch_jobs\\W+(is|are)\\W+not\\W+used"], "counter": ["queued comes from fleet-status (the live process list); ingest_batch_jobs is not used for it.", "It is derived from count_unprocessed_photos > 0; never from ingest_batch_jobs."], "pass_example": "The `queued` state comes from an open, unexpired `ingest_batch_jobs` database row (`.claude/rules/ingest.md:29`). It must **never** be derived from the `/workers/fleet-status` API endpoint — that endpoint's response is not authoritative for batch job state."}
{"id": "detect-stacks-empty-list", "area": "maintenance", "prompt": "What happens if detect_stacks or run_stacking is called with photo_ids=[] (an empty list), and why does that matter?", "any": [["(whole|entire|full)\\W+library", "no\\W+scope", "unscoped", "all\\W+photos", "every\\W+photo"], ["wipe", "\\bclear", "\\breset", "\\blost\\b", "delet", "destroy"]], "reject": ["\\b(is|it's)\\W+(just\\W+)?a\\W+no-?op\\b", "nothing\\W+(is\\W+|gets\\W+)?(wiped|cleared|re-?detected|happens|changes)", "\\b(does|do)\\W+nothing", "(it|detect_stacks|run_stacking)\\W+(just\\W+)?returns?\\W+(immediately|early)"], "facts": "photo_ids=[] is read as 'no scope', i.e. the WHOLE library, not as no photos. A stage that passed [] would re-detect over the entire library and wipe/clear existing stacks (this has happened).", "counter": ["An empty list is a no-op: nothing is re-detected and nothing wiped, so the library is safe.", "It raises ValueError for an empty list, so no stacks are lost."], "pass_example": "With `photo_ids=[]`, **`detect_stacks` returns an empty list** (line 142–143 catches `len(photos) < 2`), and then `run_stacking` **never calls `save_stacks`** because of the guard at line 621: `if not dry_run and stacks:`. Since `stacks` is empty, the scoped clearing logic never runs — the database is not modified.\n\n**Why it matters:** The scoped clear (line 324–330 in `save_stacks`) prevents accidental full-library wipes when running stacking on a subset. But if you pass `photo_ids=[]` expecting to clear that empty scope, the intent is silently skipped. The `run_incremental_stacking` function guards against this (line 523) with an explicit `if scope:` check before calling `detect_stacks`, and its comment (lines 524–525) warns: \"NEVER pass an empty list: detect_stacks reads photo_ids=[] as 'no scope' → whole library.\" That phrasing is misleading — the bug is that `photo_ids=[]` results in no stacks *and no save attempt*, leaving stale data untouched. The fix in `run_stacking` would be to call `save_stacks` even when `stacks` is empty, so the scoped clear always executes."}
{"id": "geocode-oom-per-row", "area": "maintenance", "prompt": "Why did the geocode maintenance stage OOM-kill the NAS container, and what two fixes made it fit in memory?", "any": [["per.?row", "dict", "745"], ["_MIN_POPULATION", "population"], ["stream", "StringIO", "f\\.read"]], "reject": ["added\\W+swap", "(raise|raised|increase|increased)\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,3}(memory\\W+limit|mem_limit)\\b(?!.{0,20}(instead|not))"], "facts": "RGeocoder.load keeps one Python dict per row, so the row count is the memory bill, which OOM-killed the NAS container. Fixes: (1) filter by population with _MIN_POPULATION = 1 (drop population-0 hamlets), (2) stream the CSV via RGeocoder(stream=f) instead of io.StringIO(f.read()), which held the CSV twice.", "counter": ["It loaded a per-row dict for every city; the fixes were adding swap and raising the container memory limit.", "The CSV was 2 GB; the fix was to filter by country code and cache results in Redis."], "pass_example": "The geocode maintenance stage OOM-killed the NAS because the cost was **per-ROW, not the KDTree**: `RGeocoder.load()` kept one Python dict per row at ~745 bytes — 6.58M rows = **4.9 GB** — plus `get_rich_geocoder` did `io.StringIO(f.read())`, holding the 370 MB CSV *twice* as UCS-2 text = 1.44 GB (peak ~6.3 GB vs 4.5 GB free, `exitCode=137`).\n\nTwo fixes (both needed):\n1. **`_MIN_POPULATION = 1`** in `reverse_geocoder.py` dropped class-P rows with population 0 (4.72M of 5.2M hamlets), reducing 6.58M → 1.86M rows and 4.9 GB → ~1.4 GB (named POIs kept by feature code).\n2. **Stream the CSV** — replaced `io.StringIO(f.read())` with `RGeocoder(stream=<file object>)` where `load()` iterates via `csv.DictReader` line-by-line.\n\nResult: 1.47 GB peak, 6.4 s completion (fixed in `53f167e`)."}
{"id": "vec0-orphans", "area": "database", "prompt": "Why do orphan rows accumulate in clip_embeddings and face_encodings when photos or faces are deleted, and what cleans them?", "expect": ["cleanup-orphans"], "any": [["vec0", "virtual\\W+table"], ["(no|not|n't|without|lack|ignor)\\w*\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,4}(cascade|foreign)", "(cascade|foreign\\W+key)\\w*\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,6}(doesn't|don't|not|never|aren't|isn't|can't|cannot|won't)"]], "reject": ["(vec0|virtual\\W+tables?)\\w*\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,2}(do|does)\\W+support\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,2}(cascade|foreign)"], "counter": ["vec0 virtual tables do support ON DELETE CASCADE, but it is disabled here for speed; run cleanup-orphans.", "Deletes run in a transaction that is rolled back; run cleanup-orphans."], "pass_example": "Orphans accumulate because **vec0 virtual tables can't participate in foreign-key constraints**. When photos or faces are deleted via `DELETE FROM photos` / `DELETE FROM faces`, SQLite's `ON DELETE CASCADE` never reaches `clip_embeddings` or `face_encodings`. \n\nThey're cleaned by `db.cleanup_vec_orphans()` (db.py:1986), which deletes rows with `photo_id` / `face_id` values that no longer exist. This runs:\n1. **Automatically** during `dedup_photos` stage in the maintenance sweep (maintenance.py:685)\n2. **On-demand** via `photosearch cleanup-orphans` CLI command (cli.py:2231)\n3. **Dry-run** during `photosearch verify` diagnostic sweep (maintenance.py:1015)\n\nSafety: photos and faces have `AUTOINCREMENT`, so orphan IDs are never reissued—deletion is safe."}
{"id": "api-intent-headers", "area": "patterns", "prompt": "When a Claude session calls the photosearch HTTP API directly with curl, which headers must it send and why?", "expect": ["X-Photosearch-Source", "X-Photosearch-Intent"], "reject": ["X-Photosearch-\\w+\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,4}(are|is)\\W+(optional|legacy|deprecated|not\\W+(needed|required))", "^\\W*none\\b"], "counter": ["None are required; X-Photosearch-Source and X-Photosearch-Intent are optional legacy headers.", "Authorization: Bearer <token> and Content-Type: application/json."], "pass_example": "Two headers are required on every curl call to the photo-search HTTP API:\n\n1. **`X-Photosearch-Source: claude`** — identifies the caller as Claude\n2. **`X-Photosearch-Intent: <why>`** — states the reason for the call (e.g., `debug-faces`, `check-ingest-queue`)\n\n**Why:** Intent tracking allows the API to log and audit which operations are happening and why, supporting observability and debugging. See **CLAUDE.md point 9** and the request-logging logic in `web.py:request_intent._RULES`. MCP tool calls fill the `intent` argument automatically."}
{"id": "sharpness-error-vs-absent", "area": "workers", "prompt": "In the sharpness backfill, what is recorded when a file fails to decode, versus when the file is simply not present on this machine?", "any": [["(stored|recorded|written|saved)\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,6}(sharpness_json|error)|error\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,6}(stored|recorded|written|saved)", "sharpness_json", "sharpness_version\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,3}set", "never\\W+re-?decode|not\\W+re-?decoded"], ["not_local", "records\\W+nothing|nothing\\W+(is\\W+)?(recorded|written|stored)|no\\W+row|writes\\W+nothing|not\\W+written"]], "reject": ["both\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,2}record\\W+nothing", "error\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,4}(?<!never )(?<!not )(?<!n't )(retried|re-?decoded)\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,2}(next|later|again)"], "facts": "A decode error is STORED (sharpness NULL, error in sharpness_json, sharpness_version set) so the bad file is never re-decoded. A file simply absent on this machine records nothing (reported as not_local) and writes no row.", "counter": ["Both record nothing; a decode error is retried on the next run.", "A decode error writes sharpness = 0; an absent file writes sharpness = NULL with error 'missing'."], "pass_example": "From `.claude/rules/workers.md` (lines 47):\n\n**File fails to decode:** `sharpness` is set to NULL, error is recorded in `sharpness_json`, and `sharpness_version` is set. This ensures the file is never re-decoded.\n\n**File is not present:** Nothing is written — the backfill only runs on the NAS (where originals exist); on the replica every photo is marked `not_local`."}
{"id": "mirror-fields-needs-nas", "area": "replica", "prompt": "After a sync-mode re-run from the image view on the replica, why might the result show mirrored:0, and what fixes it?", "expect": ["mirror-fields"], "any": [["404", "older\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,3}(image|NAS|build|version|than\\W+M28)", "(re)?deploy\\w*\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,2}NAS|NAS\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,3}(re)?deploy", "NAS\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,6}M28"]], "reject": ["redeploy\\W+(only\\W+)?the\\W+replica", "(the\\W+)?NAS\\W+is\\W+fine"], "facts": "The replica's mirror step calls NAS GET /api/photos/{id}/mirror-fields; a NAS image older than M28 returns 404, so the result shows mirrored:0. Fix: redeploy (deploy) the NAS first, then re-run.", "counter": ["Because the replica wasn't redeployed after M28; redeploy the replica (the NAS is fine).", "mirror-fields only copies changed columns, and nothing changed; re-run with force."], "pass_example": "The `/mirror-fields` endpoint (photosearch/rerun.py:469) doesn't exist on NAS images predating M28, returning 404. When `mirror_photos()` encounters this (line 476), it counts the photo as missing and returns `mirrored:0`. The fix is to redeploy the NAS with M28 code—until then, the authoritative write succeeds (line 49, docs/notes/frontend.md) and the replica catches up on the next `sync-replica.sh` run. Check `PHOTOSEARCH_NAS_URL` is set in the replica container environment if the endpoint exists but mirroring still fails."}
